@@ -18,6 +18,8 @@ Owner skill: `skill://ts-conventions`. The merge-request page's pure core (plan
 
 Discussions (request `docs/slices/request-mr-discussions.md` rules 2, 3, 5, design `docs/slices/briefs/mr-discussions-design.md` §1.3, §1.4, §1.8, plan §2 #163–#164): `OpenDiscussion` (a `DiscussionPlace` and its `last` comment, a `DiscussionNote`) is structural, so the window's domain type passes in unchanged. The turn rule: the viewer's turn when the last comment's author is not the viewer, an unknown author included; "waiting" when the viewer wrote it. The viewer is a username or null (nobody: every discussion is the viewer's turn); the window passes its `me`, review mode its reviewer. `discussionTurns` orders the viewer's turn first, then the waiting ones, newest last comment first within each, equal times in GitLab's order; `turnCounts` counts both. `Viewer` is whose turn the words speak for: `me { username | null }` or `person { username, name }`; `viewerOf(review, me, rows)` gives `me` for no review, Me, or a person whose username is `me`, else the person named by `displayName` of the first row reviewer with that username, else the username. Words: `DISCUSSIONS_LABEL` "Open discussions", `turnText(turn, viewer)` "Your turn" / "Anna Berg's turn" / "Waiting for an answer", `turnSummaryText(counts, viewer)` "2 of 5 wait for you", "1 of 5 waits for Anna Berg", "No open discussions" when there are none, `placeText` "Overview", `path:line` or the path alone, `noteAuthorName` the display name or `UNKNOWN_AUTHOR` "Someone", `agoText`. `noteExcerpt`: the body trimmed, its first three lines (`\n` or `\r\n`), at most 280 code points; when anything was left out it is cut there, end-trimmed, plus "…". Plain text: markup and capitals stay as typed.
 
+The viewer's possessive is `viewerPossessive(viewer, at)` alone ("Your"/"your" by `at`, a person "Anna Berg's"): `turnText` reads it at `start`, `reviewSortedText` and `reviewFailedText` `inside`.
+
 Review mode (request `docs/slices/request-mr-review-mode.md` rules 1–3, 5, 6, design `docs/slices/briefs/mr-review-mode-design.md` option A §1.1–1.5): `ReviewFor` is Me or a person, never "None"; it is `Filters.review` (null: the normal order). `reviewing(who)` is Reviewer = who included, Draft ready, every other filter off; every other edit spreads the filters, so it keeps `review`; `NO_FILTERS` has none, so Clear filters ends it; `isFiltered` counts it. `stored` writes it as a fifth key `review`, `"me"` or `"user:<username>"` (the person words), only while it is set, so the four other keys are exactly what an older yamt reads; "none", junk or a non-string reads as off. `reviewChoices` is the picker: the Reviewer filter's choices without "None", in their order, the chosen one `included`. `reviewOrdered(rows, reviewer, dueOf, detailsOf)` (`reviewOrder`): the normal order (`byDueDate`); the first row in it whose `ReviewDetails` are `failed` gives `failed` with that row and the window's own failure value, the rows left in the normal order; else one stable sort: due rows with at least one discussion that is the reviewer's turn first (keeping the normal order), then the rest by fewest waiting on the author (`turnCounts`; `none` and `loading` count as no discussion), and `loading` is how many rows have not arrived. Words: `reviewSortedText` "Sorted for your review: due with your turn first, then fewest waiting on the author" (a person: "Sorted for Anna Berg's review: due with their turn first, …"), plus " (the discussions of 1 merge request are still loading; it moves when they arrive)" or "… of 3 merge requests … they move …"; `reviewFailedText` "Not sorted for your review: the discussions of !633 could not be read. <reason>".
 
 Job log (request `docs/slices/request-ci-job-log.md` rules 4–6, design `docs/slices/briefs/ci-job-log-design.md` §1.2, §1.7–1.9, plan §2 #159): `jobLogView(raw)` turns the raw `/trace` text into `LogLine`s of styled `LogRun`s as GitLab's job page shows it (`lib/gitlab/ci/ansi2json/converter.rb`, `parser.rb`, `style.rb`, `line.rb`; bold from `ansi2html.rb`), in one scan: a break is `\r*\n`; the 32-character timestamp prefix is stripped from every line when the first line has it, and a line whose prefix ends in `+` continues the one before (only the `\n` goes, a `\r` before it then clears); `section_start`/`section_end` markers vanish and end a line that has text; a lone `\r` clears the line; only `ESC[…m` (SGR) is applied, by GitLab's exact-text code table (`sgr.ts` `penAfter`: `01` is nothing, `38;5;n` an indexed colour, `38;2;…` no truecolor, its numbers read as codes); every other escape, and C0 controls but tab, vanish; an escape cut off at a line's end drops the rest; 30–37 show bright while bold; the style carries across lines; a line without text is not shown; neighbouring text in equal styles is one run. Run text is text, never markup (RK57). `text` is the shown lines joined by `"\n"`: what the page's `search/` matcher runs over; `hitPlaces` maps its `{ line, from, to }` hits onto runs (a `from` at a run's end starts the next run, a `to` there ends in that run; a hit outside the view is left out). `basicColour` turns a 256-colour index into one of the 16 `AnsiColour`s, 16–255 the nearest on xterm's default palette (the triples classify, never paint: the page maps `AnsiColour` to the `--color-ansi-*` tokens). `jobLogSearch`: `firstHit` the last match, `steppedHit` wraps both ways, `hitCountText` "3 of 17", `NO_RESULTS`; for the page's highlights only the lines near the view count: `firstLineBelow(count, edge, y)` the first line whose growing `edge` is past `y` (binary search), `placesOnLines(places, first, end)` the places on lines `first` to `end` exclusive (binary search on the line order `hitPlaces` gives).
